---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/content';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="About & contact"
  ><section class="container page-intro">
    <span class="eyebrow">About & contact</span><h1>
      Michael Simkin.<br /><em>Independent researcher.</em>
    </h1><p>{site.introduction}</p>
  </section><section class="container about-layout">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>My research</h2><p>
        I’m self-taught. Research is both my professional work and a hobby, and my interests have
        changed over time.
      </p><p>
        In my youth, I represented Israel at the 1999 International Mathematical Olympiad and won a
        bronze medal. Since then, I’ve worked in several startups on different ideas.
      </p><p>
        I worked in computational geometry before moving into computer vision and AI research. More
        recently, I’ve worked freelance with several startups on medical research. My main Conway’s
        Game of Life research phase was from 2014 to 2019, alongside that work. Lately, I’ve been
        exploring AI-assisted mathematical proofs.
      </p>
      <h2>Conway’s Game of Life and other cellular automata</h2><p>
        My interest in Life began with an article about Andrew Wade’s original
        {' '}<a href="https://conwaylife.com/forums/viewtopic.php?t=399">Gemini</a>. One of my first
        results was
        {' '}<a href={href('articles/serizawa-geminoid/')}>Serizawa Geminoid</a>, a smaller, more
        efficient construction in another rule. I later built
        {' '}<a href={href('articles/geminoid-particles/')}>Geminoid Particles</a>, then used the
        same construction technique years later in {' '}<a href={href('articles/remini/')}>Remini</a
        >.
      </p><p>
        This journal includes the {' '}<a href={href('articles/simkin-glider-gun/')}
          >Simkin Glider Gun</a
        >,
        {' '}<a href={href('articles/caterloopilar/')}>Caterloopillar</a>,
        {' '}<a href={href('articles/remini/')}>Remini</a>, and
        {' '}<a href={href('articles/switch-engine-ping-pong/')}>switch-engine ping-pong</a>. The
        articles document the constructions and their attribution, including shared contributions.
      </p><p>
        Follow this work through the{' '}
        <a href={href('interests/game-of-life/')}>Conway’s Game of Life</a>{' '}and{' '}
        <a href={href('interests/cellular-automata/')}>CA Research</a>{' '}interest pages.
      </p><h2>Building the tools to ask a question</h2><p>
        Curiosity is a starting point. Experimentation and engineering are how I turn an idea into
        something I can test and develop.
      </p><p>
        For the glider-gun search, I wanted a simple approach: place a few potential catalysts and
        search the combinations. Making that practical meant first building the infrastructure,
        LifeAPI and CatForce.
      </p><p>
        Each article identifies its status—from an experiment to a demonstrated construction or
        research preprint—and links to the public material behind it.
      </p><h2>Explore the work</h2><p>
        Start with an{' '}<a href={href('interests/')}>interest</a>, or browse the{' '}<a
          href={href('#articles')}>recent articles</a
        >. The categories are different ways into the same collection.
      </p><h2>Contact</h2><p>
        Contact me by email. My public code and project documentation are on GitHub.
      </p><div class="about-links">
        <a href={site.github}>simsim314 on GitHub ↗</a>{
          site.email && <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
        }{site.arxiv && <a href={site.arxiv}>arXiv profile ↗</a>}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section></Base
>
